From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Levente Botos <botos.levente2007@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 11:21:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Network pads: phones and other local programs as controllers

macOS cannot create a virtual HID gamepad without an entitlement Apple restricts, so a phone cannot be a controller the system lists. A host-side input source can: DM_NETPAD=<port> listens on 127.0.0.1:<port> for UDP datagrams ("P <pad> <buttons> <lx> <ly> <rx> <ry>", "L <pad>") and gives each pad that sends a player of its own.

A pad takes its player from the same slot table as a controller (pad_slots.h), so it never shares a player with a controller or the keyboard, gets its player back when it returns, and follows DM_KBD_PLAYER=own, DM_PAD_PRESS_TO_JOIN and DM_PAD_MERGE. It publishes through controller_publish_player, so the controller applet's prompt counts it, A and B close that prompt, and the window's focus releases it. A pad silent for 2 s leaves, so a dead sender cannot hold a button.

Off unless DM_NETPAD names a port, and bound to 127.0.0.1 only: any local process can send input to a loopback port. The launcher setting "Phone controllers" (Mac only) sets DM_NETPAD=47600.

netpad.h is pure (parsing, clamping, join / leave / expiry as functions of an explicit time) and tested by t_netpad.c. t_netpad_mac.m sends real datagrams to netpad_mac.m, and t_controllers_mac.m covers the slot, merge, press-to-join, full-table and focus rules. Not tested: a phone driving a real game, rumble and motion (not sent), iOS (the _mac files stay out of it).

Front-ends: new: DM_NETPAD (network pads on 127.0.0.1) - a front end can offer the toggle and run a sender

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Upstream-Status: pending
Reason: Phones as controllers: network pads (DM_NETPAD) for the Mac host, meant for seedds/nxnative
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#1122

