+## Network pads: phones and other programs
+
+`DM_NETPAD=<port>` makes the game listen on `127.0.0.1:<port>` for UDP datagrams and gives each pad that sends a player of its own, as a physical controller has ([`netpad.h`](../dm/host/netpad.h), `netpad_mac.m`). The launcher's **Phone controllers** setting sets `DM_NETPAD=47600` ([Settings](settings.md#phone-controllers)). The engine ships no sender: a phone's web page and a bridge on the Mac, or any program, can be one. This is local input for one Mac, not networked play ([MULTIPLAYER.md](MULTIPLAYER.md)).
+
+| Message | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `P <pad> <buttons> <lx> <ly> <rx> <ry>` | the pad's whole state. `<pad>` 1 to 8 is the sender's own number for the pad. `<buttons>` is a decimal number of the 16 button bits of `dm_input.h` (A 1, B 2, X 4, Y 8, … Down 32768; other bits are dropped). Sticks run from -32767 to 32767 (larger values are clamped), +y is up |
+| `L <pad>` | the pad has left: everything is released and its player is free |
+
+- One datagram is one message, plain ASCII, fields split by spaces, a trailing newline is fine. Anything else is ignored whole, including a datagram over 96 bytes.
+- A sender repeats `P` about once a second while nothing changes. A pad that sends nothing for 2 seconds has left too, so a sender that dies cannot leave a button held.
+- The pad number is not the player. The pad takes a player like a controller does (`pad_slots.h`): the lowest free one, player 1 when it is free, its old player when it comes back. It never shares a player with a controller or the keyboard, `DM_KBD_PLAYER=own` keeps player 1 for the keyboard, and `DM_PAD_PRESS_TO_JOIN=1` makes a pad a player at its first message with a button pressed. With `DM_PAD_MERGE=1` every pad is merged into player 1. The log names each one (`network pad 2: player 3`), and a pad that finds all eight players taken is logged once and joins when one is free.
+- A pad counts for the controller applet's prompt ("N players wanted"), and its A and B close it. While the window is not in focus its buttons are released, as a controller's are.
+- Not covered: rumble and motion are not sent back, and a pad is not a Joy-Con (no rail buttons, Shake or touch).
+
+```sh
+echo "P 1 1 0 0 0 0" | nc -u -w1 127.0.0.1 47600       # pad 1 presses A (a player joins)
+echo "P 1 0 32767 0 0 0" | nc -u -w1 127.0.0.1 47600   # releases it, left stick right
+echo "L 1" | nc -u -w1 127.0.0.1 47600                  # pad 1 leaves
+```
+
+**Security.** A loopback port is open to every program on this Mac, and any of them can press buttons in the game. That is why it is off unless the player turns it on. Nothing outside the Mac can reach it. A sender on a phone reaches it through a program on the Mac that listens on the network and forwards to the port, which is the player's choice to run.
+
